NEW FIRMWARE FOR SISMO SC-MB
----------------------------
Since the original SISMO Firmware has a few flaws, I have reverse engineered
the components of the board including all I2C boards and written my new
firmware for it with the following bugs corrected:

- true 1 millisecond polling of GPIO's compared to 2-5 ms in the original
- even with connected I2C boards a 1 ms polling is guaranteed. This makes
  sure that optical encoder steps are not missed.
- data of inputs is only sent if an input changes. The original board
  sends inputs every 2-5 ms inependent of changes in input states. This
  simply floods the network with unnessesary data
- data of analog inputs is median filtered and only sent if it does not
  change due to input noise. The original board sends analog inputs every
  2-5 ms
- Option for diagnostic messages via the UDP protocol (enable by setting
  the VERBOSE option in the ini file to 2 or higher). You can then see
  if the SISMO SC-MB has trouble accessing I2C Boards or other error occur

The UDP protocol with the new firmware is the same as described by SISMO
for their original firmware.

The firmware is located in Firmware.X. This is a MPLAB X IDE Project.
You should be able to load and build it there without any troubles.
It should be possible to import it into VSCode, but I have not tested
it since the used PIC18F86J60 has no MCC support in VSCode. This
makes initial development difficult. The ready-to-use firmware
should however be usable in VSCode.

The programming goes via the 4 Pin Header of the SISMO SC-MB connected
to a PicKit Programmer. I have tested it with the PicKit 5.

The following wiring will be needed:

PICKIT		SC-MB		Function
1		1		VPP
3		4		GND
4		3		PGD
5		2		PGC
7		PIN 37 (RC6)	UART TX (You have to solder it to the RC6 
				elements of the board if you want diagnostics
				via the UART line onto the serial monitor
				of MPLAB X IDE

Compared to the original firmware the configuration of the board does
work via the command line tool writeconfig, which accesses the respective
.cfg file in inidata/

The command goes like this
./writeconfig NAME-OF-CONFIGFILE IP-ADDRESS PORT

where
- NAME-OF-CONFIGFILE is the prefix of the config file where you defined the board configuraion
- IP-ADDRESS is the Ip Address of the board (per default to 192.168.1.150)
- PORT is the port of the board (per default 1024)

If you have no idea at which IP Address your board is configured, use the
script scanports.sh to find hosts that have the port 1024 open. You should
see your SISMO Board somewhere (make sure to turn off all other SISMO boards).

